# 3️⃣ Promises – Deep Dive

## What is a Promise?

An object representing the eventual completion or failure of an async operation.

### States

- pending
- fulfilled
- rejected

---

## Internal Working

- Promise constructor runs synchronously
- resolve() / reject() schedule microtasks
- State becomes immutable after settling
- .then() always returns a new Promise

---

## Promise Chaining

```js
doTask()
  .then(result => result + 1)
  .then(value => {
    throw new Error("Failure");
  })
  .catch(err => console.log(err.message));
```

Errors propagate until caught.

---

## Promise Methods

### 1️⃣ Promise.all()

- Runs in parallel
- Fails fast (rejects immediately)

```js
Promise.all([p1, p2, p3])
  .then(console.log)
  .catch(console.error);
```

---

### 2️⃣ Promise.allSettled()

- Waits for all
- Returns status objects

```js
Promise.allSettled([p1, p2]);
```

Returns:
```js
[
  { status: "fulfilled", value: ... },
  { status: "rejected", reason: ... }
]
```

---

### 3️⃣ Promise.race()

- First settled (resolve/reject) wins

---

### 4️⃣ Promise.any()

- First fulfilled wins
- Rejects only if all fail

---

# 4️⃣ Promise Polyfill – Promise.all

```js
function myPromiseAll(promises) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    let results = [];
    let completed = 0;

    promises.forEach((p, index) => {
      Promise.resolve(p)
        .then(value => {
          results[index] = value;
          completed++;

          if (completed === promises.length) {
            resolve(results);
          }
        })
        .catch(reject);
    });
  });
}
```

